-- title:
--   Adic points of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient and their twisted fibres
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$. The arithmetic base consists of: a commutative domain $\mathcal O$ of characteristic zero which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi$, the hypothesis that $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the requirement $\#(\mathcal O/\pi)=r$, and the requirement that $(r)=(\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$; together with a characteristic zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The unramified coefficient ring is a characteristic zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $F_r$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $F_r(x)\equiv x^{r}\pmod\pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`), so that $F_r$ lifts the $r$-power Frobenius of the residue field. A monoid homomorphism $\mathrm{vdet}\colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g)=n$ exactly when $\det g=u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$; that is, $\mathrm{vdet}$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   The group-theoretic data are a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma\colon G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma\le G$ such that: $\Gamma$ contains an element $z$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix and $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma z)=2$ (`hcent`), and an element $w$ with $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma w)=1$ (`hodd`); and a subgroup $\Gamma'\le G$ whose members are, by `hΓ'`, exactly the $x\in\Gamma$ with $\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma x)$ even. Furthermore $\rho\colon G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the homomorphism induced by $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the vertices of the Bruhat–Tits tree of $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ (homothety classes of $\mathcal O$-lattices, [`LT.LatticeTree.Vertex`](def/LatticeTreeOrbital.html#L349)) with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, in the form: there is a finite set $S$ of vertices such that every vertex can be carried into $S$ by an element of $\rho(\Gamma')$ (`hcocpt`).
--
--   On the geometric side a scheme $\mathcal X$ and a morphism $f\colon\mathcal X\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ are given, assumed proper and flat. For an $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, $(\mathrm{Scheme.nilpPoints}\,f)(B)$ denotes the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\mathcal X$ whose composition with $f$ is the structure morphism $\operatorname{Spec}B\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, functorially in $\mathcal O$-algebra maps via $\operatorname{Spec}$, and $(\mathrm{AlgFunctor.prod}\,(\mathrm{AlgFunctor.corep}\,O^{\mathrm{nr}})\,(\Omega_{K_0,\pi}))(B)$ denotes the set of pairs $(\psi,P)$ with $\psi\colon O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map and $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$ (a choice of line in each lattice's base change, with the monotonicity, homothety, invertibility and nondegeneracy clauses of `DeligneDatum`), functorially by base change of lines.
--
--   The uniformisation datum is a family $\Theta$ assigning, to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from such pairs $(\psi,P)$ over $B$ to $B$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, subject to four clauses. `hΘnat`: $\Theta$ is natural in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps. `hΘinv`: for $\gamma\in\Gamma$ and pairs $x,x'$ over $B$ satisfying $\mathrm{OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct}$ for $\sigma\gamma$ — namely $x'_1$ is the Frobenius twist $F_r^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma\gamma)}$ of $x_1$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$ — one has $\Theta(x')=\Theta(x)$. `hΘfib`: for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent in $k$, and every $\psi\colon O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to k$, every $k$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ is $\Theta(\psi,P)$ for some Deligne datum $P$ over $k$, and $\Theta(\psi,P)=\Theta(\psi,P')$ holds if and only if some $\gamma\in\Gamma$ relates $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ by the twisted action. `hΘuniv`: $\Theta$ is universal among such families, i.e. for every scheme $T$ with a morphism $t\colon T\to\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps from pairs over $B$ to $B$-points of $T$ over $\mathcal O$ which is natural in $B$ and invariant under the twisted action of $\Gamma$, there is a family $u$ of maps from $B$-points of $\mathcal X$ to $B$-points of $T$ (for $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent) which is natural in $B$, satisfies $u\circ\Theta=\rho'$, and is unique with these two properties.
--
--   Finally the frame: $C$ is an algebraically closed field with decidable equality, a $K_0$-algebra, carrying a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero and complete for it; $R$ is a commutative ring which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a subring-like source of scalars for $C$, with compatible scalar towers $\mathcal O\to R\to C$ and $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$; $\varpi$ is a pseudo-uniformizer, i.e. an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and with the scaling property that every nonzero $a\in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^{N}\le v(a)\le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; and `hF : IsAdicFrame π ϖ R` asserts that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image exactly $\{c\in C: v(c)\le 1\}$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation at most $1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the images of $\pi$ and $\varpi$ in $C$ agree. An $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0\colon O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to R$ is fixed. For $n\in\mathbb N$, $\mathrm{modPow}\,\pi\,R\,n$ denotes $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, and $\mathrm{AdicPoint}\,K_0\,\pi\,R$ denotes the type of compatible systems $x=(x_n)_{n}$ of Deligne data $x_n$ over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, compatible in the sense that the base change of $x_{n+1}$ along $R/(\pi^{n+2})\to R/(\pi^{n+1})$ is $x_n$. Write $\psi_{0,n}$ for the reduction of $\psi_0$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$.
--
--   The conclusion is the conjunction of the following two assertions.
--
--   (i) For every morphism $p\colon\operatorname{Spec}R\to\mathcal X$ whose composition with $f$ is the structure morphism of $R$ over $\mathcal O$, there exists an adic point $x$ of $\mathrm{AdicPoint}\,K_0\,\pi\,R$ such that for every $n$ the reduction of $p$, i.e. $\operatorname{Spec}(R/(\pi^{n+1}))\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ followed by $p$, equals the morphism underlying $\Theta$ evaluated over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ at the pair $(\psi_{0,n},x_n)$.
--
--   (ii) For all adic points $x,x'$: the equalities $\Theta(\psi_{0,n},x_n)=\Theta(\psi_{0,n},x'_n)$ hold for every $n$ if and only if there exists a single $\gamma\in\Gamma$ such that for every $n$ the pairs $(\psi_{0,n},x_n)$ and $(\psi_{0,n},x'_n)$ are related by $\mathrm{OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct}$ for $\sigma\gamma$ over $R/(\pi^{n+1})$, i.e. $\psi_{0,n}$ is its own Frobenius twist by $-\mathrm{vdet}(\sigma\gamma)$ and $x'_n$ is the pullback of $x_n$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the $R$-integral point statement for a scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ presented as a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient: the $\pi$-adic uniformisation $\Theta$ of $\mathcal X$ by Deligne data on the formal upper half plane, with unramified coefficients twisted by Frobenius, is surjective on points valued in the valuation ring $R$ of a complete algebraically closed $C$ once these are read level by level modulo $\pi^{n+1}$, and its fibres at a fixed coefficient map $\psi_0$ are exactly the orbits of the twisted $\Gamma$-action. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_adicPoint_to_sections_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_adicPoint_to_sections_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient), the step which converts adic points into sections of the quotient.
